speechtext:
I have been able hurriedly to go over this order in council, P.C. 2607, which was passed on Wednesday, April 1, 1942, which would be the effective date of the beginning of the United Kingdom war financing measure. Under paragraph 1, which includes the regulations, it is provided that payments out of the consolidated revenue fund shall in the first instance be made as accountable advances to the government of the United Kingdom, and it concludes by stating that there shall be received a requisition "together with an undertaking to account for such advances in the form set forth in the next succeeding paragraph hereof." Then section 2 is a further regulation which provides that as soon as practicable after the end of each month the minister shall obtain from the High Commissioner of the United Kingdom a statement showing the amounts expended during the preceding month out of the accountable advances " made under paragraph 1 hereof " and the purposes for which such amounts were expended. I quote:
Such statement shall show separately the amounts expended in respect of:
(a)	Foodstuffs;
(b)	Raw material;
(c)	Munitions of war and other expenditure by defence departments;
(d)	Other expenses incurred in Canada
and shall show such further details as the minister may from time to time require.
It is apparent, from a casual reading of that, that the intention was that the advances should be accountable and should be accounted for. If that was followed out, I do not understand the minister's difficulty. I am not saying this in any critical sense. So far as I am concerned, I am satisfied that the money went to Great Britain and was used as part of a common pool for the common purpose of assisting in winning the war. I am wondering what difficulty the minister has had, having regard to the procedure indicated in paragraphs 1 and 2 of the order in council.
[Mr. Ilsley.J
